        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Normandy landings</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">6 June 1944 (D-Day), after a postponement of 24 hours from the 5 June first chosen</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">An 80-kilometre stretch of the Normandy coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, divided into the sectors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword, with airborne landings inland</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a>, with General Bernard Montgomery commanding the land forces, nearly 160,000 troops crossing the Channel on the day; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under Hitler, with Field Marshal Gerd von Rundstedt as Supreme Commander West and Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-rommel">Erwin Rommel</a> commanding Army Group B</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> established a beachhead at each of the five sites on the first day, but Carentan, Saint-Lô, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> stayed in German hands; the five beachheads were not connected until 12 June.</span></div>
