    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The landings were the largest seaborne invasion in history: a fleet of 6,939 vessels from eight navies, nearly 160,000 troops across the Channel on D-Day and 875,000 landed by the end of June.</li><li>Allied casualties on the first day were at least 10,000, with 4,414 confirmed dead; German casualties are estimated at 4,000 to 9,000. Omaha, with its high cliffs, had the most Allied casualties.</li><li>Many of the first-day objectives were not met: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, a major objective, was not completely captured until 21 July, and the Germans never achieved Hitler's stated aim of throwing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> back into the sea.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The day was chosen because the tides, the hour and the moon allowed only a few dates a month; when storms forced a postponement from 5 June, the next suitable dates were two weeks later, and a major storm struck the Normandy coast from 19 to 22 June.</p></aside>
    </section>

