    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">May 1943</span><span class="rf-text">The decision to undertake a cross-channel invasion within the next year is taken at the Trident Conference in Washington in May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1943</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> and Montgomery first see the plan and insist on widening the landings to five divisions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 4 June <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> meets Group Captain James Stagg, whose forecast of improving weather leads him to go ahead on 6 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 6 June airborne troops land after midnight and the amphibious landings on the five beaches begin about 06:30</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 12 June the five beachheads are connected</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1944</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> is completely captured</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1944</span><span class="rf-text">German forces retreat east across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, closing Operation Overlord</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="normandy-landings" data-v="7a9a5b6a41" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Airborne troops land behind the beaches</b> Pathfinders of the US airborne divisions arrived at 00:15, and British glider troops took the Caen canal and Orne bridges at 00:16. Many paratroopers landed far from their drop zones.</li><li><b>The flanks are taken</b> On the western side the 82nd Airborne captured the crossroads at Sainte-Mère-Église within two hours of landing. On the eastern side the 6th Airborne held the bridges over the Caen canal and the Orne.</li><li><b>The fleet shells the coast</b> Warships bombarded the ground behind the beaches in the dark and, from 05:50, pre-assigned targets on the beaches themselves. The assault divisions crossed in landing craft, which strong winds blew east of their intended positions.</li><li><b>Utah and Omaha are landed on</b> The 8th Infantry Regiment of the 4th Division landed first at Utah at 06:30, about 1,800 metres south of its intended place. At Omaha, where the 1st and 29th Divisions came ashore, obstacles were largely undamaged and fire from the cliffs was heavy.</li><li><b>Gold, Sword and Juno are landed on</b> Landings at Gold were set for 07:25 and infantry began disembarking at Sword at 07:30. At Juno the landing was delayed by choppy seas, and the men arrived ahead of their supporting armour and suffered many casualties.</li><li><b>Combat teams leave Utah Beach</b> Combat teams began to leave Utah at around 09:00, some infantry wading through flooded fields. They skirmished with the 919th Grenadier Regiment through the day, and the 4th Division made a rendezvous with the airborne troops.</li><li><b>Barely 600 men reach the heights at Omaha</b> Exit from Omaha was possible only through five heavily defended gullies, and by late morning barely 600 men had reached the higher ground. By noon the Americans were able to clear some lanes on the beaches.</li><li><b>Fortified towns are cleared at Gold, Juno and Sword</b> At Gold, Juno and Sword the fortified towns were cleared in house-to-house fighting. The 2nd Battalion, King's Shropshire Light Infantry advanced on Caen to within a few kilometres of the town but had to withdraw for lack of armour support.</li><li><b>The 21st Panzer Division counter-attacks</b> The 21st Panzer Division attacked between Sword and Juno and nearly reached the Channel. It met stiff resistance from the British 3rd Division and was soon recalled to the area between Caen and Bayeux.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the German Army invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in June 1941, the Soviet leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> pressed his new allies to create a second front in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In May 1942 the Soviet Union and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> announced a full understanding on the urgent tasks of creating one that year, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> to postpone it, since even with American help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> lacked adequate forces. The western Allies instead fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, winning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> by mid-1943 and invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in July and the Italian mainland in September.</p><p>The decision to cross the Channel within the next year was taken at the Trident Conference in May 1943, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in November 1943 Roosevelt and Churchill promised Stalin a second front in May 1944. Planning was limited by the number of landing craft, most of which were committed in the Mediterranean and Pacific. Four sites were considered: Brittany, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula">Cotentin Peninsula</a>, Normandy and the Pas-de-Calais. The first two were peninsulas, which the Germans could cut off at a narrow isthmus. The Pas-de-Calais, the closest point to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, was the most heavily fortified and offered few chances to expand. Normandy would allow threats to Cherbourg, to the ports of Brittany and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and its lack of ports was to be met by artificial Mulberry harbours.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> and Montgomery thought the first plan, three divisions on a 40-kilometre front, too small and insisted on five divisions and three airborne ones. The extra landing craft this needed delayed the invasion from May to June.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Normandy landings&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Ken Ford, Overlord: The D-Day Landings (Osprey, 2009)</li><li>Antony Beevor, D-Day: The Battle for Normandy (Viking, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower.jpg" alt="Dwight D. Eisenhower" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dwight_D._Eisenhower,_official_photo_portrait,_May_29,_1959_(cropped)(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">White House, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Allied command was General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> at the head of the Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, with General Bernard Montgomery commanding the 21st Army Group. The First Army, under Lieutenant General Omar Bradley, landed about 73,000 men, 15,600 of them airborne, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> and Omaha. The Second Army, under Lieutenant General Sir Miles Dempsey, put ashore 83,115 men, 61,715 of them British, at Gold, Juno and Sword. The fleet, under Admiral Sir Bertram Ramsay, numbered 6,939 vessels from eight navies, including 1,213 warships and 4,126 landing craft, with 195,700 naval personnel. Specialised tanks nicknamed Hobart's Funnies, and Duplex-Drive amphibious tanks, supported the landings.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had fifty divisions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Combat losses, above all on the Eastern Front, meant its soldiers were on average six years older than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>', and many in Normandy were Ostlegionen with captured equipment and little motor transport. Seven of the eleven panzer or panzergrenadier divisions in France were not fully operational or only partly mobile in early June. Rommel ordered stakes, tripods, mines and obstacles on the beaches and tripled the mines along the coast, though he reported construction in some areas only 18 per cent complete.</p><p>The German commanders disagreed over the armoured reserves: Rommel wanted tanks close to the coast, Rundstedt and Geyr wanted them concentrated further back. Hitler left three Panzer divisions under Geyr, gave Rommel control of three, and kept four himself.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Normandy landings&#8221; cites where it covers Deception plans, German order of battle, Cotentin Peninsula, Grandcamps Sector:</p><ul><li>Ken Ford, Overlord: The D-Day Landings (Osprey, 2009)</li><li>Antony Beevor, D-Day: The Battle for Normandy (Viking, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> deceived the Germans about the date and place of the landing through Operation Bodyguard, including a fictitious army group under Patton, and on the eve of the invasion dummy paratroopers and strips of metal foil were used to suggest further landings. The first Allied action was the glider capture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> canal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a> river bridges, afterwards Pegasus Bridge, at 00:16. American airborne landings began with pathfinders at 00:15, but cloud scattered the paratroopers; after 24 hours only about a third of those dropped were under their divisions' control. Sainte-Mère-Église was the first town liberated.</p><p>Bombing began about midnight, but was largely ineffective at Omaha, where low cloud and fear of hitting the troops led many bombers to release late. The naval bombardment of the beaches began at 05:50. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> the 8th Infantry Regiment landed at 06:30, about 1,800 metres from the intended place, and the 4th Infantry Division landed 21,000 troops for 197 casualties. Omaha was the most heavily defended beach, held by the 352nd Infantry Division rather than the single regiment expected. Currents and fire cost the landing force dearly, 27 of 32 amphibious tanks sank, and by late morning barely 600 men had reached the higher ground.</p><p>At Gold, Juno and Sword the landings began from 07:25. Towns were cleared in house-to-house fighting, specialised tanks disabled the strongpoints at Le Hamel and elsewhere, and at 16:00 the 21st Panzer Division counter-attacked between Sword and Juno before meeting stiff resistance.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Normandy landings&#8221; cites where it covers German order of battle, Cotentin Peninsula, Grandcamps Sector, Allied order of battle:</p><ul><li>Ken Ford, Overlord: The D-Day Landings (Osprey, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nearly 160,000 troops crossed the Channel on D-Day, and 875,000 men had landed by the end of June. Allied casualties on the first day were at least 10,000, with 4,414 confirmed dead, and the Germans had 4,000 to 9,000 casualties, whether killed, wounded, missing or captured. Civilian casualties on D-Day and the day after are estimated at 3,000. The Germans had ordered French civilians not essential to the war effort to leave potential combat zones in Normandy.</p><p>The build-up of troops and the secure bridgehead came with fewer casualties than expected, but the plan had called for the capture of Carentan, Saint-Lô, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> on the first day, with the beaches other than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> linked, and none of these objectives was achieved. At Omaha the objectives were reached by 9 June. The five beachheads were connected on 12 June, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> held a front about 97 kilometres long and 24 kilometres deep. Caen was not completely captured until 21 July, and German forces retreated east across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> on 30 August 1944, closing Operation Overlord.</p><p>The Allied victory is put down to several factors: the German defences were only partly finished, the deceptions left the Germans to defend a huge coastline, and Rommel and other commanders and troops were absent because of the forecast storms. The Allies held air supremacy, bombers and the French Resistance disrupted transport, specialised armour worked well except at Omaha, and German command was indecisive and overcomplicated.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Normandy landings&#8221; cites where it covers Naval losses, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Ken Ford, Overlord: The D-Day Landings (Osprey, 2009)</li><li>Antony Beevor, D-Day: The Battle for Normandy (Viking, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-speer.jpg" alt="Albert Speer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Albert Speer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146II-277,_Albert_Speer_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Binder (maybe Atelier Binder after Alexander Binders death in 1929), CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The history of the landings rests on the records of both sides and on later testimony. The record of the airborne preparations includes the BBC war correspondent Robert Barr's description of the paratroopers boarding their aircraft, and the German side is represented by the memoir of the armaments minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Albert Speer</a>, published in 1969, which recalls the German high command's fear of airborne attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>.</p><p>The figures for the dead and wounded are ranges and estimates. German casualties are put at 4,000 to 9,000, and Allied casualties on the first day at least 10,000, of whom 4,414 are confirmed dead. Casualties for individual beaches are estimates too, for instance about 1,000 at Gold, 961 at Juno and as high as 1,000 at Sword, and the 3,000 civilian casualties are also an estimate. A 1965 report by the Counter-insurgency Information Analysis Center gives the results of the French Resistance's sabotage, saying that 52 locomotives were destroyed in the south-east on 6 June and that Normandy was isolated as of 7 June.</p><p>The naval planning is judged by later writers: the historian Correlli Barnett called it a never surpassed masterpiece of planning. The reasons for the Allied success listed by historians include German weakness, the deceptions, air supremacy and the weather.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Normandy landings began the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> and laid the foundations for the Allied victory on the Western Front. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, who had promised Stalin a second front at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, now had one, and the Germans never achieved Hitler's stated aim of throwing them back into the sea, either on D-Day or at any time afterwards.</p><p>Memorials and museums mark the coast. At Omaha Beach parts of the Mulberry harbour are still visible, and the Normandy American Cemetery and Memorial lies nearby at Colleville-sur-Mer. Pointe du Hoc is little changed from 1944, with its bomb craters and most of its concrete bunkers. At Pegasus Bridge the bridge was replaced in 1994 by one of similar appearance, and the original stands in the grounds of a nearby museum. Sections of Mulberry Harbour B remain in the sea at Arromanches, and the Longues-sur-Mer battery is well preserved. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juno-beach">Juno Beach</a> Centre opened in 2003, funded by the Canadian federal and provincial governments, France and Canadian veterans. The British Normandy Memorial above Gold Beach, designed by Liam O'Connor, opened in 2021, and two German military cemeteries are nearby.</p><p>In July 2026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the Beaches of the D-Day Landings a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 6 June 1944 Allied troops landed from the sea and the air on five beaches in Normandy, the largest seaborne invasion in history. They secured a foothold on the first day but not their main objectives, and the German retreat across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> on 30 August closed Operation Overlord.</em></p>

